steps.message:
Because `NeedsWrappers` targets .NET 10, you do not need a custom clock interface — use the built-in **`TimeProvider`** abstraction. That is the recommended way to make `DateTime.UtcNow` deterministic.

### 1. Register it in DI

```csharp
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

### 2. Inject it into ReportGenerator

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var now = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime;   // was DateTime.UtcNow, Kind stays Utc
        var timestamp = now.ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}\nGenerated: {now:O}\nMachine: {Environment.MachineName}";
        // Use timestamp for the path and content for File.WriteAllText.
    }
}
```

### 3. Pin time in tests with FakeTimeProvider

Add the **`Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`** NuGet package to the test project, then use `FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;
using Xunit;

public class ReportGeneratorTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void GenerateReport_StampsTheReportWithThePinnedTime()
    {
        var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider();
        fakeTime.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, TimeSpan.Zero));
        var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);

        var path = generator.GenerateReport("sales");

        // the emitted stamp must equal the pinned instant, not the wall clock
        Assert.Equal("sales_20240102_030405.txt", Path.GetFileName(path));
    }
}
```

The production method captures `GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime` once and reuses it for both the filename and the `Generated:` content, so no `DateTime.UtcNow` read remains. The test constructs the fake, pins it to a known instant, exercises `GenerateReport`, and asserts both emitted timestamps equal the pinned value — real time-dependent assertions, not just building the fake. `FakeTimeProvider.Advance(...)` lets you also drive elapsed-time logic. Keep `TimeProvider.System` registered in production so behaviour is unchanged.
